{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgENfdDkFy6zQwJqYcJJe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012z54Wx2fMTEYBWx689ca71","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"find notes tests __pycache__ -type f | head -50 && echo --- && for f in $(find notes tests -type f -name '*' | head -30); do echo \"== $f\"; head -c 3000 \"$f\"; echo; done 2>/dev/null | head -300; echo ---; python3 -m unittest discover -s tests 2>&1 | tail -20","description":"Inspect notes, tests, and run the existing test suite"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4680,"cache_read_input_tokens":21152,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4680},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4fa32509-ec40-4f05-a206-85bc20173244","uuid":"514a5f15-51c1-465b-9a65-794c5a1dac66","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:41:49.189Z","request_id":"req_011CgENfcy7s5kvkaBpxaTo3","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012z54Wx2fMTEYBWx689ca71":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-6v2SJG && find notes tests __pycache__ -type f | head -50 && echo --- && for f in $(find notes tests -type f -name '*' | head -30); do echo \"== $f\"; head -c 3000 \"$f\"; echo; done 2>/dev/null | head -300; echo ---; python3 -m unittest discover -s tests 2>&1 | tail -20","description":"Inspect notes, tests, and run the existing test suite"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_012z54Wx2fMTEYBWx689ca71":{"cwd":"/private/tmp/h-6v2SJG"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012z54Wx2fMTEYBWx689ca71","type":"tool_result","content":"notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\nnotes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\nnotes/a-stranger-could-like-my-private-tasks.md\ntests/test_api.py\n__pycache__/api.cpython-314.pyc\ntests/__pycache__/test_api.cpython-314.pyc\n---\n== notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `<path>` instead — guaranteed\n  everywhere.\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\n\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\nclick-to-copy version inside the app too, but the embed is just markdown, so it\nworks anywhere markdown does. The badges are already on my own repo's README,\ndoing their little double duty.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a web app/API you control that stores a public, per-item stat\n(reproductions, stars, downloads, streak — anything already public), and a way\nto add a public HTTP route.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which stat to surface, where it lives (your DB/\nstore), your brand colors, and your public base URL.\n\nSteps:\n1. Add a **public** `GET /badge/{id}.svg` route that looks up the item's stat.\n   No auth — the badge must be embeddable by anyone; only expose already-public\n   numbers.\n2. Return a two-segment \"shields-style\" SVG: a dark label segment (your app\n   name) + a colored value segment (the stat). Compute each segment's width from\n   its text length so it never clips. Set `Content-Type: image/svg+xml`.\n3. Draw any icon (✓, ★) as an inline `<path>`, never a unicode character — text\n   glyphs depend on the viewer's fonts and show as boxes in some renderers. Keep\n   the SVG fully self-contained (no external fonts or images).\n4. Add a short `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=300`). GitHub's image\n   proxy will cache it regardless; this just keeps the source cheap.\n5. Hand users the embed line — the trailing link\n== notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n---\n\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\n\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\ndatabase, no build step.\n\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\n\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\nconfusion.\n\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\n\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\nthe email are the confirmation, not the fetch.\n\nSimple things should stay simple.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a static site you can edit and deploy, and a Google account.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which spreadsheet to write into (or permission\nto create one), the address to notify, and the list of fields to collect.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create a Google Sheet for submissions. Its ID is the long string in the URL\n   between `/d/` and `/edit`.\n2. In that sheet choose Extensions, then Apps Script. Write a `doPost(e)` that\n   parses `e.postData.contents` as JSON; returns success immediately and writes\n   nothing if the honeypot field is non-empty; rejects missing required fields;\n   takes a `LockService.getScriptLock()` before writing so two submissions in\n   the same second cannot interleave; appends a row via\n   `SpreadsheetApp.openById(<id>).getSheets()[0].appendRow([...])` with a\n   timestamp first; releases the lock; then sends a notification with\n   `MailApp.sendEmail`. Truncate every field to a maximum length before writing.\n3. Deploy, New deployment, type \"Web app\", Execute as **Me**, Who has access\n   **Anyone**. Authorise it. Copy the URL ending in `/exec`.\n4. In the page, add the form with the real fields, plus a hidden input that no\n   human can see or tab into (`tabindex=\"-1\"`, `aria-hidden=\"true\"`) as the\n   honeypot, and\n== notes/a-stranger-could-like-my-private-tasks.md\n---\ntitle: A stranger could \"like\" my private tasks — the access rule my API forgot\ndate: 2026-08-21\ntags: [api-testing, access-control, idor, authorization, claude-code, agent-skills]\ntype: success\nstatus: n/a\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# A stranger could \"like\" my private tasks — the access rule my API forgot\n\nPlain version: an API bug where the app correctly stops you from *seeing* someone\nelse's private thing, but forgets to stop you from *touching* it. You can't open\nmy neighbour's locked drawer — but you can still slip a sticky note under the lid.\nThat class of hole (IDOR / broken object-level authorization) is invisible from\nthe outside and easy to leave in one endpoint out of twenty.\n\nI wanted to check the backends of two of my own apps — a story library and a\nsocial to-do app — without hand-writing a test plan. I installed\n[**paranoid-qa**](https://github.com/akovalion/paranoid-qa), a Claude Code skill\nby **Aleksei Kovalev**, whose whole philosophy is \"evidence or it didn't happen\":\nthe agent only marks a\ncheck Pass or Fail from a real observed response, never a guess. Its `testing`\nskill ships a backend checklist — status codes, auth, injection, and the\nauthorization checks that matter for anything multi-user.\n\nFirst a safe unauthenticated pass: are the right things 401, do bad payloads\nreturn 400 instead of 500, does an obvious SQL-injection string reach anything.\nBoth APIs were clean there.\n\nThe interesting part needed two real accounts. Account A created a **private**\ntask; account B (a stranger) tried to read, edit, delete, comment on, complete,\nnudge, and like it. Everything returned a correct 403 — **except two endpoints**:\n\"like\" and \"register a view\" both returned 200 and actually mutated A's private\ntask. B changed a like count and a view count on something B was forbidden from\neven opening. Small blast radius, but it's a real authorization gap, and it was\none guard missing on two endpoints while the other twenty had it.\n\nI fixed it (add the same visibility check the comments endpoint already used),\nadded regression tests, and the whole run was non-destructive — every probe task\ndeleted afterward. Thank you [Aleksei](https://github.com/akovalion/paranoid-qa)\nfor open-sourcing the skill; the \"check *every* endpoint, not just the obvious\none\" discipline is exactly what caught it.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: **Claude Code** (https://claude.com/claude-code); an API of your\nown to test; two test accounts on it (one is enough for the read/validation\nchecks — you need two to prove cross-user access); short-lived auth tokens for\neach (never a long-lived secret).\n\nYour agent will need from you: the API base URL, how to get an auth token for\neach account, and which actions are safe to run against the environment (prefer\nstaging; if it must be production, keep it read-only + self-owned and clean up).\n\nSteps:\n1. Install the skill. Check your agent's inventory first: look for a `testing`\n   skill under `~/.claude/skills\n== tests/test_api.py\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport unittest\nimport urllib.error\nimport urllib.request\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))))\nimport api  # noqa: E402\n\nALICE = \"tok_alice_4f8c2e71b0\"\n\n\nclass ApiTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.srv = api.make_server(0)\n        cls.base = \"http://127.0.0.1:%d\" % cls.srv.server_address[1]\n        threading.Thread(target=cls.srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n\n    @classmethod\n    def tearDownClass(cls):\n        cls.srv.shutdown()\n        cls.srv.server_close()\n\n    def setUp(self):\n        api.seed()\n\n    def call(self, method, path, body=None, token=ALICE):\n        data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else None\n        req = urllib.request.Request(self.base + path, data=data, method=method)\n        if token:\n            req.add_header(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \" + token)\n        req.add_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n        try:\n            with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as r:\n                return r.status, json.loads(r.read())\n        except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:\n            return e.code, json.loads(e.read() or b\"{}\")\n\n    def test_needs_token(self):\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"GET\", \"/tasks\", token=None)[0], 401)\n\n    def test_list_and_read_public(self):\n        code, body = self.call(\"GET\", \"/tasks\")\n        self.assertEqual(code, 200)\n        self.assertIn(\"Water the office plants\", [t[\"title\"] for t in body[\"tasks\"]])\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"GET\", \"/tasks/2\")[0], 200)\n\n    def test_create_edit_delete(self):\n        code, t = self.call(\"POST\", \"/tasks\", {\"title\": \"Order coffee beans\", \"visibility\": \"public\"})\n        self.assertEqual(code, 201)\n        code, t = self.call(\"PATCH\", \"/tasks/%d\" % t[\"id\"], {\"title\": \"Order more coffee beans\"})\n        self.assertEqual((code, t[\"title\"]), (200, \"Order more coffee beans\"))\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"DELETE\", \"/tasks/%d\" % t[\"id\"])[0], 200)\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"GET\", \"/tasks/%d\" % t[\"id\"])[0], 404)\n\n    def test_social_actions_on_public_task(self):\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"POST\", \"/tasks/2/comments\", {\"text\": \"on it\"})[0], 201)\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"POST\", \"/tasks/2/like\")[1][\"likes\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"POST\", \"/tasks/2/view\")[1][\"views\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"POST\", \"/tasks/2/nudge\")[1][\"nudges\"], 1)\n        self.assertTrue(self.call(\"POST\", \"/tasks/2/complete\")[1][\"done\"])\n\n    def test_bad_input(self):\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"POST\", \"/tasks\", {\"visibility\": \"public\"})[0], 400)\n        self.assertEqual(self.call(\"GET\", \"/nope\")[0], 404)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n\n== tests/__pycache__/test_api.cpython-314.pyc\n+\u000e\r\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000���j�\n\u0000\u0000�\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000�v\u0001\u0000\u0000�\u0000^\u0000R\u0001I\u0000t\u0000^\u0000R\u0001I\u0001t\u0001^\u0000R\u0001I\u0002t\u0002^\u0000R\u0001I\u0003t\u0003^\u0000R\u0001I\u0004t\u0004^\u0000R\u0001I\u0005t\u0006^\u0000R\u0001I\u0007t\u0006]\u0002P\u0010\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000P\u0013\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000^\u0000]\u0001P\u0010\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000P\u0015\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000]\u0001P\u0010\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000P\u0015\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000]\u0001P\u0010\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000P\u0017\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u00